Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time, the disciples came to Jesus. They asked him, "Who is the most important person in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to him. He stood the child among them.
Jesus said, "What I tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you don't do this, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Until one becomes empty of pride like this child, one is the least important in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Whoever accepts such a little child in my name accepts me.
And if ever someone among the young, who believe in me in a transgression, it is better for him that a large millstone be hung about his neck and he be drowned in the depths of the sea.
How terrible it is for the world because of the things that lead people into sin! Things like this must come. But how terrible it is for those who cause it!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one hand or one foot than to enter hell with two hands and two feet. In hell, the fire burns forever.
If your eye causes you to sin, pluck it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hellfire. The story of the lost sheep.
Look, don't despise one of these little ones. This is what I tell you. Their angels in heaven can go at any time to see my Father who is in heaven.
<.>
What do you think? Suppose a man owns 100 sheep, and one of them goes astray. Doesn't he leave the 99 sheep on the hills? Doesn't he go and search for the one that went astray?
What I am going to tell you is true. If that sheep is found, he will be happier with it than with the ninety-nine who have not strayed.
The same is true of your Father in heaven. He causes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous. If you love those who love you, what credit is that to you? Even the tax collectors do the same. And if you greet only your brothers, what more are you doing than others? Even the Gentiles do the same. You therefore must be perfect, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. Keep it between the two of you. If he listens to you, you have him back.
But what if he does not listen to you? Take along one or two others. The scriptures say: "One witness is not sufficient to convict a criminal, nor is one witness sufficient to convict a criminal. Every matter must be established by the testimony of two or three witnesses." (Deuteronomy 19:15)
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then tell the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Then do not treat him as a brother. Treat him as you would treat a lawless or tax collector person.
What I will tell you is the truth. What you close up on earth will be closed up in heaven. What you open up on earth will be opened up in heaven.
Again, this is what I tell you. Suppose two of you on earth agree about anything you ask for. Then my Father in heaven will do it for you.
"Wherever two or three are gathered in my name, I am there among them." The slave who had no mercy.
Peter came to Jesus. And he asked him, "Lord, how often shall I forgive my brother if he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The kingdom of the heavens is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants. He called one of them and asked him, 'How much do you owe my master?' The servant said, 'A hundred measures of oil.' The king said to his servant, 'Go quickly to my other servants; tell them, "I am about to bring a charge against you and your servants. You, in turn, are to bring a charge against still other servants. This is what my master, the king, wants.'' The king continued, 'Therefore, settle your accounts quickly with your fellow servants, so that your own servants may also deal with you quickly.' The servant who had been forgiven the great debt went out and found one of his fellow servants who owed him a small amount. He grabbed him and demanded, 'Pay me what you owe!' His fellow servant fell to his knees and begged him, 'Be patient with me, and I will pay you back.' But his fellow servant refused. Instead, he went to the master and reported, 'This fellow servant of mine owes me a small amount, which he refuses to pay. And he even went to the master and slandered me.'" The master became angry and handed the fellow servant over to the torturers until he should pay back all that he owed. So my heavenly Father will also do to each of you, if, from your heart, you do not forgive your brother his sins. (Matthew 18:23-35, paraphrased and expanded for clarity based on the provided text and common interpretations of the parable, without adding external information or engaging in doctrinal debate.)
When the king started doing this, a man who owed him millions of dollars was brought to him.
The man could not pay. A command was issued by his master. The man, his wife, his children, and all that he possessed were to be sold to pay his debt.
The server fell to his knees before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will pay everything."
The lady felt sorry for him. She forgave him his debts and set him free.
But then that valet came out and found one of the other valets to whom he owed a few dollars. He grabbed him and began to strangle him. "Pay me back what you owe me!" he said.
The other slave fell to his knees. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will pay you back."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and threw the man into prison. The man would have stayed there until he could repay his debt.
The other servants saw what happened. It greatly disturbed them. So they went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the Lord called the first servant. He said to him, "You wicked servant, I forgave you all that debt because you begged me.
Haven't you thought that you should have mercy on the other servant, just as I have shown mercy to you?
In his anger, his master handed him over to the torturers. He was to be punished until he had repaid all his debts.
Thus will my Father who is in heaven treat each of you if you do not forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for just any reason?"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read that from the beginning the Creator made them male and female?" (Genesis 1:27)
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave to his wife; and they shall become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
They are no longer two, but one. What God has joined together, let no one separate.
They asked, "Then why did Moses command a man to give his wife a certificate of divorce and send her away?"
Jesus answered, "Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because you were stubborn. But it was not like that from the beginning."
Here is what I say to you. Whoever divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. A man is not permitted to divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If such is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining single. Only those who have been helped to live without marriage can accept it.
Some men are unable to have children because they were born that way. Some have been made that way by other people. And others have made themselves that way in order to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever can accept living in this way must do so." Young children are brought to Jesus.
Some people brought their little children to Jesus. They wanted him to place his hands on the children and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them. For the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he went from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man came to Jesus. And he asked him, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to obtain eternal life?"
Jesus said, "Why do you ask me about what is good? There is only one who is good. If you want to enter into the kingdom, then obey the commandments."
"Which?" asked the man. Jesus said, "Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not bear false witness."
«Honor your father and your mother.» (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) and «You shall love your neighbor as yourself.» (Leviticus 19:18)
The young man said, "I have obeyed all those commandments, so what else must I do?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he left sadly. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "What I am about to tell you is true. It is difficult for the rich to enter the kingdom of heaven.
Again I tell you, it is difficult for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven. It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven.
When the disciples heard this, they were greatly astonished. And they said, "Who then can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "For mortals, this is impossible, but for God, all things are possible."
Peter answered and said to him, "Behold, we have left everything and followed You! What then will be our reward?"
Jesus said to them, "What I am about to tell you is true. When all things are renewed, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. Then you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones. You will judge the twelve tribes of Israel."
Everyone who has left houses or families or fields for my sake will receive a hundred times more. And they will inherit eternal life.
But many of the first shall be last; and many of the last shall be first.
The Story of the Workers in the Vineyard
The kingdom of the heavens is like a man, a landowner, who went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
At about nine in the morning, he went out again. He saw others standing in the market doing nothing.
He said to them, "Go you also and work in my vineyard. I will give you what is right."
They left. "He went out again around noon and at three o'clock and did the same thing.
Around five o'clock he went out and found others still standing around him. He asked them, "Why do you stand here all day long without doing anything?"
"Because no one has hired us," they answered. He said to them, "You go also and work in my vineyard."
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the manager of the workers. He said, "Call the workers and give them their pay. Start with the last ones you hired, and then go to the first ones you hired."
The workers who were hired around five o'clock arrived. Each of them received the usual day's wage.
When the time came for those who had been hired first to be paid, they thought that they would be taking more. As a result, each one of them also took the wage for the day.
When they took possession, they began complaining about the owner.
They said, "These recently hired people only worked for one hour. You paid them the same as you paid us. We were the ones who did most of the work and were under the scorching sun all day."
The owner answered one of them, "My friend," he said, "I am dealing fairly with you. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?"
Take your money and leave. I want to give those I last hired the same salary I gave you.
Do I not have the right to do what I want with my own money? Do you feel cheated because I gave generously to others?
Those who are last will be first, and those who are first will be last. Jesus once again tells about his coming death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
He said, "We are going up to Jerusalem. The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the teachers of the law. They will condemn him to death."
Then they will hand him over to people who are not Jews. They will mock him and flog him. They will crucify him on a cross. On the third day, he will rise from the dead!" Or ask Jesus a favor
The mother of the sons of Zebedee came to Jesus with her sons. She knelt down and asked him a favor.
Jesus said, "What do you want?" She said, "Promise me that one of my two sons will sit at your right hand in your kingdom. And promise that the other will sit at your left hand."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup of suffering that I am to drink?" They said to him, "We can."
Jesus said to them, "You will surely drink from my cup. But it is not for me to say who will sit at my right hand or at my left. These places are prepared for those whom my Father has prepared for me."
The other ten pupils heard about this. They were angry with the two brothers.
Jesus brought them together. He said, "You know about the rulers of the Gentiles. They lord it over their people. Their high officials give them orders.
Do not be like this. Rather, whoever wants to be important among you must be your servant.
He who wants to be first must be your servant.
Be like the Son of Man. He came not to be served, but to serve others. He came to give his life as a ransom for many. Two blind men receive their sight.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A great crowd followed them.
Two blind men were sitting by the roadside. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they called out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
The crowd ordered them to stop. They told them to be silent. But the two men cried out even louder, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called to them, "What do you want me to do for you?" he asked.
They said, "Lord, we want to be able to see."
Jesus felt deep concern for them. He touched their eyes. Immediately they could see. And they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
When they all approached Jerusalem, they came to Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two disciples.
He said to them, "Go to the village before you. As soon as you reach it, you will find a donkey tied up. Its saddle is with it. Untie them and bring them to me."
If someone tells you something, say that the Lord needs them. The owner will send them immediately.
This happened so that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled. It says,
Say to the city of Zion's daughter, "Behold, your king is coming to you, just, and having salvation, lowly, and riding on a donkey, even on a colt, the foal of a donkey." (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
Bring the donkey and the colt. They placed their coats on them. Then Jesus sat on the coats.
Very large crowds spread out and put their coats on the road. Others cut branches from trees and placed them on the road.
Some people went ahead of him, and others followed. And everyone cried out, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up. The people asked, "Who is this?"
The multitude answered, "This is Jesus. He is the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple area. He began driving out those who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of those who were exchanging money. He also overturned the benches of those who were selling doves.
He said to them, "It is written, 'The Lord has said, "My house shall be called a house of prayer."' (Isaiah 56:7) But you have made it 'a den of thieves.' " (Jeremiah 7:11)
The blind and the lame came to Jesus at the temple. There he healed them.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonderful things he did. They also saw the children in the temple area shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" When the chief priests and teachers of the law saw this, they began to be very angry.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked Jesus. "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you not read in the Scriptures: 'The children and the infants come and praise you'? 'From the mouths of infants and nursing babies you have established praise for yourself.'" (Psalm 8:2-4)
Then Jesus left the people and went out of the city to Bethany. He spent the night there. The fig tree withered.
Early in the morning, Jesus was going back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the road. He came up to it but found nothing on it but leaves. Then he said to it, "May you never bear fruit again!" Immediately the tree withered.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed. They said, "How did the fig tree dry up so quickly?"
Jesus answered, "What I tell you is true. You must believe and not doubt. Then you can do what was done to the fig tree. And you can say to this mountain, 'Go and throw yourself into the sea.' And it will be done.
"If you believe, you will receive whatever you ask for when you pray." Jesus' authority is being questioned.
Jesus entered the temple courts. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him. They asked, "By what authority are you doing these things?" "And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered, "I will also ask you one question. If you tell me the answer, then I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From where did the baptism of John come? From heaven or from men?" And they began to reason among themselves, saying, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say to us, 'Why then did you not believe him?'"
But what if we say, "of the men"? We fear people. Everyone thought that John was a prophet.
They answered Jesus, "We do not know." Jesus said, "Nor will I tell you by what authority I do these things." The Parable of the Two Sons
What do you think about this? There was a man who had two sons. He went to the first son and said, "My son, go and work today in the vineyard."
"I won't," the son answered. But he later changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. The son answered, "I will, sir." But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did what his father wanted?" "The first," they answered. Jesus said to them, "The truth is, the tax collectors and prostitutes are entering the Kingdom of God ahead of you.
John came to show you the right way to live. And you did not believe in him. But tax collectors and prostitutes believed. You have seen this. But even then you did not turn away from your sins and did not believe in him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. A man who owned some land planted a vineyard. He put a fence around it. He dug a pit for a winepress in it. He also built a watchtower. He rented the vineyard to some farmers. Then he went on a trip.
When the harvest time approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. He told the servants to gather his share of the fruits.
But the tenants seized his servants. They beat one of them. They killed another. They stoned the third to death.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at first. The tenants treated them in the same way.
Finally, he sent them his son. He said, "They will respect my son."
But the tenants saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will get all the owner's property someday. Come, let's kill him. Then it will all be ours."
And they took him and cast him out of the vineyard. And they killed him.
When the vineyard owner returns, what will he do with these tenants?
They said, "These evil people will destroy." Then he will rent the vineyard to other tenants. They will give him his share of the crop at harvest.
Jesus said to them, "Have you not read what was spoken by the Lord, saying, 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner. This is the Lord's doing, and it is marvelous in our eyes'? (Psalm 118:22,23)"
Therefore I tell you this. The kingdom of God will be taken from you. And it will be given to people who produce its fruit.
Anyone who crashes into that stone will be shattered into pieces. But the stone will crush anyone who falls on it.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They knew that he was speaking about them.
Therefore, they looked for a way to arrest him. But they were afraid of the crowd. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus told them more stories. He said,
Thus is the kingdom of heaven. A king prepared a wedding dinner for his son.
Servants were sent to those who had been invited to the dinner. The servants told them to come. But they refused.
Then he sent other servants and said, "Tell those invited, 'I have prepared my dinner. I have killed my oxen and fattened pigs for this day. All is ready. Come to the wedding feast.' "
But the people did not pay attention. One of them went to his field. And another went to his work.
The rest seized his servants. They treated them harshly and then killed them.
The king became very angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The wedding banquet is ready. But those I invited were not worthy of attending."
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner everyone you can find.
The servants went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they could find, whether good or bad. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He noticed a man there who was not wearing wedding clothes.
He said to him, "My friend, how did you get in here without the wedding clothes?" The man could not find anything to say.
Then the king said to his servants, "Bind his hands and feet. Throw him outside into the darkness. There people will weep and grind their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and plotted how they might entangle Jesus in His words.
They sent their disciples to him. They sent the Herodians with them. They said, "Teacher, we know that you are a man of integrity. You teach the way of God in truth. You are not swayed by others, telling you what to do or say. You do not care about their status."
So then tell us, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus knew their evil plans. He said, "You hypocrites! Why are you trying to trap me?"
"Show me the coin that people use to pay the tax." They brought him a silver coin.
He asked them, "Whose picture is this? And whose words are these?"
They said to him, "To Caesar." Then he said to them, "Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."
When they heard this, they were astonished. So they left him and went away. Marriage, when the dead rise.
On that same day, the Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people rise from the dead.
They said, "Teacher, this is what Moses told us. If a man dies and has no children, his brother must marry the widow. He must have children to carry on his brother's name."
There were seven brothers among us. The eldest married and died. Since he had no children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second brother, the third brother, and so on, until the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
If the dead rise, by which wife will they be married? For they all were married to her.
Jesus answered, "You are wrong, because you do not know the Scriptures. And you do not know the power of God.
When the dead rise, they will not get married. Nor will their fathers give them away in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the dead being raised? Have you not read what God has said to you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham. I am the God of Isaac. And I am the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) He is not the God of the dead. He is the God of the living.
When the crowd heard this, they were amazed at what he was teaching. The most important commandment.
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees could not answer Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them was an expert in the law. He tested Jesus with a question.
He said to him, "Teacher, which commandment is the most important in the law?"
Jesus answered, " 'Love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind.' " (Deuteronomy 6:5)
This is the first and most important commandment.
And the second is like it: 'Love your neighbor as yourself.' (Leviticus 19:18)
All that is written in the law and the prophets is based on these two commandments." Son of whom is Christ?
The Pharisees met together. Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is he? They said to him, The son of David.
He said to them, "Then why does David call him 'Lord'? The Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said,
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet." (Psalm 110:1)
If David called him "Lord," how can he be David's son?
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any more questions.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
He said, "The scribes and Pharisees sit on the seat of Moses."
You must obey them. Do everything they command you to do. But do not do what they do. For they do not practice what they preach.
They tie heavy loads and place them on the shoulders of others. But they themselves do not mind lifting a finger to move it.
All they do is for the others to see. On their foreheads and arms they wear small boxes containing texts from the Bible. They make the boxes very wide. And they make the tassels on their coats very long.
They like to sit in the place of honor at banquets. They also like to get the most important seats in synagogues.
They like to be welcomed into the markets. They like it when people call them "Rabbi".
But you should not call him 'Rabbi'. You have only one teacher, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth "father." You have one Father, and He is in heaven.
You should not be called "Teacher." You have one teacher, the Christ.
The most important person among you will be your servant.
He who exalts himself shall be abased; and he who abases himself shall be exalted.
And how terrible it will be for you, you teachers of the law and Pharisees, hypocrites! You shut the door of the kingdom of heaven in people's faces. And you yourselves do not enter. Nor do you allow those who are entering to go in."
<.>
You snakes, you brood of vipers! How can you escape being condemned to hell? For you travel over land and sea to convert one person to your religion; and then you make them twice as much a child of hell as you are yourselves.
Blind guides, woe to you! For you say, 'If anyone swears by the temple, it means nothing; but if anyone swears by the gold of the temple, he must keep his vow.'
You are blind and foolish! Which is more important? Is it the gold? Or is it the temple which makes the gold sacred?
And it also says: "If anyone swears by the altar, it means nothing. But whoever swears by the gift upon it, he must keep his oath."
O ye blind! Which is the greater, giveth, or the altar that sanctifieth the gift?
Anyone who takes an oath by the name of the altar, swears by it and by everything on it.
He who swears by the temple swears by it and by him who dwells in it.
And he who swears by the name of heaven, he swears by the name of the throne of God and by the name of him who sits upon it.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you tithe in dill, cumin, and mint, and neglect the weightier matters of the law: justice, mercy, and faithfulness. These you ought to have done, without leaving the others undone.
O ye blind guides! Ye make clean the outside of the cup and of the platter; but within, ye are full of extortion and excess.
You hypocrites, you snakes, you brood of vipers! How can you escape being sentenced to hell? For you make your outward appearance good, but inside you are full of hypocrisy and evil desires. You foolish people! You strain out a gnat but swallow a camel!
O blind Persian! First clean the inside of the cup and the saucer. Then the outside will also be clean.
You brood of vipers, you generation of vipers! How can you escape being sentenced to hell? You snakes! You are careful to appear beautiful and innocent on the outside. But on the inside, you are full of hypocrisy and evil desires. You are like tombs that are painted white on the outside. They look beautiful and impressive from a distance. But on the inside, they are full of decaying bones and other unclean things.
The same goes for you. From the outside, it looks like you're doing the right thing. But on the inside, you're filled with what's wrong. You pretend to be what you're not.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! You build the tombs of the prophets and decorate the tombs of the righteous.
And you say, "If we had been in the days of those who were before us, we would not have killed the prophets and we would not have been of the wrongdoers."
You bear witness against yourselves. You acknowledge that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
So complete the sins that those before you began!
Oh, nests of deadly vipers! How will you escape being sentenced to exile in hell?
Therefore, I am sending you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, you will crucify some of them, and you will flog others in your synagogues and pursue them from town to town.
You shall pay with your own blood for the blood of all the righteous ones that has been shed upon the earth. I mean, from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah, the son of Barachiah. Zechariah is the one whom you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I will tell you is the truth. All of this will happen to those who are living now.
O Jerusalem! O Jerusalem! You are killing the prophets and throwing stones to kill those who are sent to you. How often I have wanted to gather your people together! I wanted to be like a hen that gathers her chicks under her wings. But you would not let me!
Look, your house was left empty.
I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, 'Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord.'" (Psalm 118:26)
End Times Signs
Jesus left the temple. He was walking away when his disciples approached him. They wanted to draw his attention to the temple buildings.
"Do you see all these things?" Jesus asked. "Truly I tell you, not one stone here will be left on another. Every stone will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. There, the disciples came to him privately. They said to him, "Tell us, when will these things happen? And what will be the sign of your coming? And what will be the sign of the end of the age?"
Jesus answered, "Beware! Be careful that no one deceives you.
Many will come in my name. They will claim, "I am the Messiah!" They will deceive many people.
You will hear about wars. You will also hear people talking about future wars. Do not worry. Those things must happen. But the end is not yet come.
Nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom. There will be great earthquakes, famines and pestilences in various places.
All these are the beginning of the birth pangs.
Then they will hand you over to be mistreated and killed. All nations will hate you for my name's sake.
At that time, many will depart from their faith. They will hate one another. They will deliver one another to their enemies.
Many false prophets will appear. They will deceive many people.
Because evil is increasing, the love of most people will grow cold.
But whoever endures to the end will be saved.
This good news about the Kingdom will be preached in the whole world as a testimony to all nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the detestable thing that causes desolation." (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) In the future you will see standing in the holy place, the one who causes desolation. Let the reader understand.
Let those who are in Judaism flee to the mountains.
No one on the surface should go down to their home to get something.
No one in the field should go back to get their coat.
How dreadful it must have been in those days for pregnant women! How dreadful it must have been for nursing mothers!
I hope you don't have to flee in winter or on a Saturday.
There will be terrible suffering in those days. It will be worse than anything else from the beginning of the world until now. And there will be nothing like it again ever.
If the time were not cut short, no one would survive. But because of the chosen people of God, it will be cut short.
At that time, someone might say to you, "Look! Here is the Christ!" or "There he is!" Do not believe it.
False messiahs and false prophets will appear. They will perform great signs and wonders. They will try to deceive God's chosen people if possible.
Look, I already told you.
If someone says to you, 'He is in the wilderness,' do not go out. And if someone says, 'He is in the inner room,' do not believe it.
The lightning that comes from the east can be seen in the west. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
Vultures will gather wherever there is a dead carcass.
After the terrible ordeal of those very days, "the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light; the stars will fall from the sky, and the heavens will be shaken." (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
Then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven with power and great glory.
He will send His angels with a loud trumpet call. They will gather His chosen people from the four corners of the earth. They will bring them from one end of the heavens to the other.
Learn a lesson from the fig tree. Once its branches become soft and its leaves sprout, you know that summer is near.
In the same way, when you see all those things happening, you know the end is near. It's at the doorstep.
What I am about to tell you is a fact. The people who were living at that time will certainly not die until all those things happen.
The heavens and the earth will pass away, but my words will never pass away. Concerning that day and hour no one knows.
No one knows about that day or hour. Not even the angels in heaven know. The Son does not know. Only the Father knows.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They were doing all those things until the day that Noah entered the ark.
They had no idea what was going to happen until the flood came and swept them all away. That is how it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Two men will be in the field. One will be taken, and the other will be left.
Two women will grind using a hand mill. One will be taken and the other will be left.
Therefore, wake up! Indeed, you do not know on which day your Lord will come.
You must understand something. Suppose the homeowner knew at what time of night the thief would come. Then he would have stayed vigilant. He would not have allowed his house to be broken into.
Therefore, you also must be ready; for the Son of Man will come at an hour when you do not expect Him.
Assume a master who entrusted one of his slaves to manage the rest of the slaves in his house. The slave's job is to distribute their food to them in a timely manner. The master wants a faithful and wise slave for this matter.
It would be good for the servant if he found the lady doing his work when the master returned.
What I am going to tell you is true. His master will make him responsible for everything he owns.
But suppose that server is evil. Suppose it said to itself, "My master is away for a long time."
Suppose he begins by striking the other servants. And suppose he eats and drinks with those who drink heavily.
He will return, Master of that slave, on a day that the slave does not expect. He will return at an hour that the slave knows not.
Then the master will cut him to pieces. He will send him to the place where the protesters go. There the people will weep and gnash their teeth.
The Story of the Ten Bridesmaids
Thus shall the kingdom of heaven be at that time. And she took ten virgins, their lamps, and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were fools. Five were wise.
The ignorant ones took their lamps, but they did not take olive oil with them.
The wise ones took oil in jars with their lamps.
The groom did not come for a long time. So all the bridesmaids got tired and fell asleep.
In the middle of the night, someone shouted, "Here is the bridegroom! Come out to meet him!"
Then all the bridesmaids woke up and prepared their lamps.
The ignorant said to the wise, "Give us some of your oil, for our lamps are going out."
"No," they answered. "There may not be enough for all of us. Instead, go to those who are selling the oil. Buy some for yourselves."
So they went to buy oil. But while they were on their way, the bridegroom arrived. The bridesmaids who were ready with him entered the wedding feast. Then the door was locked.
Later, the other bridesmaids also came. They said, "Master! Master! Open the door for us!"
But he answered, "What I am going to tell you is the truth. I do not know you."
Be vigilant. You do not know the day or the hour when the bridegroom will come. The story of the three servants.
Again, this is how the kingdom of heaven will be. A man was going on a journey. He sent his servants and entrusted his property to them.
He gave one person ten thousand dollars. He gave another person four thousand dollars. He gave a third person two thousand dollars. The man gave each servant the amount of money he knew the servant could manage. Then he went on his journey.
The servant who received 10,000 dollars went immediately and put his money to work. He earned another 10,000 dollars.
The person who received 40,000 dollars won an additional 40,000 dollars.
But the man who received two thousand dollars went and dug a hole in the ground. He hid his master's money there.
After a long time, the master of these servants returned. He wanted to collect all the money that they had earned.
The man who received $10,000 brought the other $10,000. He said, "Sir, you trusted me with $10,000. Look, I have earned another $10,000."
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful in a few things, so I will put you in charge of many things. Come and share your master's joy!"
The man with 40,000 dollars also came. He said, "Sir, you trusted me with 40,000 dollars. Look, I have earned another 40,000 dollars."
His master answered and said unto him, Well done, good and faithful servant; thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will make thee ruler over many things: enter thou into the joy of thy lord.
Then the man to whom he had paid two thousand dollars came. He said, "Sir, I knew you were a hard man. You reap where you have not sown. You gather where you have not scattered."
So I was scared. I went out and buried your 2000 dollars in the ground. Look, this is what's yours.
His master answered and said unto him, O wicked and slothful servant! Thou didst know that I reap where I sow not, and gather where I have not strewed:
Well, you should have deposited my money in the bank. When I returned, I would have received it with interest.
Then his master ordered the other servants, "Take 2,000 dollars from him. Give it to the one who has 20,000 dollars.
Everyone who has will be given more; he will have more than enough. But what about the one who has nothing? Even what he has will be taken away from him.
Cast out that worthless servant into the outer darkness. There shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.' The sheep and the goats.
The Son of Man will come in his glory. And all the angels will come with him. Then he will sit on his throne in the glory of heaven.
All nations will be gathered before him. He will separate the people into two groups. He will be like a shepherd separating the sheep from the goats.
He will make the sheep to his right and the dumb (cattle) to his left.
Then the king will speak to those on his right. He will say to them, "My Father has blessed me. Come and take what is yours. It is the kingdom that has been prepared for you since the creation of the world."
I was hungry. And you gave me something to eat. I was thirsty. And you gave me something to drink. I was a stranger. And you invited me in.
I needed clothes. And you gave them to me. I was sick. And you took care of me. I was in prison. And you visited me.
Then the righteous who did good works will answer them. They will say, "Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you? Or when did we see you thirsty and give you a drink?
When did we see you a stranger and take you in? When did we see you needing clothes and clothe you?
When did we see you sick or in prison and visit you?
The King will answer, "What I tell you is the truth. Whatever you did for one of these least brothers of mine, you did for me."
Then He will say to those on His left, 'Cursed are you! Go away from Me into the fire which is prepared for the unbeliever and his demonic brothers.'
I was hungry. But you did not give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you did not give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger. But you did not let me in. I needed clothes. But you did not give me any. I was sick and in prison. But you did not take care of me.
Then they also will answer him, saying, 'Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty or a stranger or naked or sick or in prison and did not minister to you?'
He will answer: "What I tell you is the truth. Whatever you did not do for one of these least, you did not do for me."
Then they go to be punished forever. As for those who did right, they will receive eternal life.
The plan to kill Jesus.
Jesus finished saying all these things. Then he said to his disciples,
You know, Easter is in two days. The Son of Man will be delivered to be crucified on a cross.
Then the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled. They met in the palace of Caiaphas, the high priest.
They made plans to arrest Jesus cunningly. They wanted to kill him.
"But not during the feast," they said. "Or the people may become stirred up." A woman pours perfume on Jesus.
Jesus was in Bethany. He was at the home of a man named Simon, who had been suffering from a skin disease.
A woman came to Jesus with a special sealed alabaster jar containing very precious perfume. She poured the perfume on his head while he was reclining at the table.
When the students saw this, they became angry. They said, "Why is there such waste?"
The perfume could have been sold for a high price. The money could have been given to the poor.
Jesus perceived this. He said to them, "Why do you bother this woman? For she has done a beautiful thing to me.
You will always have the poor with you. But you will not always have me.
I poured perfume on my body to prepare me for burial.
What I am going to tell you is the truth. What I have done shall be heralded everywhere where this good news is preached throughout the world. It shall be told in commemoration of her." Judas agrees to hand over Jesus
One of the twelve went to the chief priests. His name was Judas Iscariot.
He asked, "What will you give me if I hand Jesus over to you?" So they counted out for him thirty pieces of silver.
Since then, Judas had been watching for an opportune time to hand Jesus over to them. The Lord's Supper
It was the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread. The disciples came to Jesus. They asked, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover?"
He answered, "Go into the city to a certain man. Tell him, 'The Teacher says: "My time is near. I will keep the Passover at your house with my disciples."'"
So the disciples did what Jesus had instructed them. They prepared the Passover meal.
When evening came, Jesus was at the table with the twelve.
While they were eating, he said, "What I am about to tell you is true. One of you will betray me to my enemies."
The disciples became very sad. One after another, they began to say to him, "Is it I, Lord?"
Jesus answered, "The one who dips his hand in the bowl with me is the one who will betray me.
The Son of Man will go, just as it is written about him. But woe to the one who betrays the Son of Man! It would have been better for him if he had not been born.
It was Judas who would betray him. He said, "Isn't it I, Rabbi?" Jesus answered, "Yes. You are the one."
While they were eating, Jesus took bread. He thanked and broke it. He handed it to His disciples and said, "Take this, eat from it. This is My body."
Then he took the cup. Thanked and gave it to them. He said, "Drink from it all of you."
This is my blood of the new covenant. It is shed for the forgiveness of many sins.
This is what I am telling you. From now on, I will not drink wine with you again until the day I drink it with you in my Father's kingdom.
Then they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus says that Peter will fail.
Jesus said to them, "In this very night, all of you will desert me. For it is written, 'The Lord said, I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered.' (Zechariah 13:7)'"
But after I am raised from the dead, I will go ahead of you to Galilee."
Peter answered, "Everyone else might leave because of you. But I will never do that."
Jesus said: "What I tell you is true, it will happen this very night. Before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times."
But Peter said, "I must die with you. I will never say that I don't know you." All the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus Prays in Gethsemane
Then Jesus went with his disciples to a place called Gethsemani. He said to them, "Sit here while I go over there and pray."
Peter took with him James and John, the sons of Zebedee. And he began to be sorrowful and very heavy.
Then he said to them, "My soul is very sorrowful. I feel that death is very near me. Stay here. Keep watch with me."
He went a little further. Then he fell on his face to the ground. He prayed, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. Yet not what I will, but what you will."
Then he came back to his disciples and found them sleeping. He said to Peter, "Could you not watch with me for one hour?"
Watch and pray. Then you will not fall into temptation when you are put to the test. The spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak.
Jesus went out to pray again. He said, "My Father, if it is possible, may this cup be taken from me. Yet not as I will, but as you will."
Then he came back. He found them sleeping again. They couldn't keep their eyes open.
So he left them and went away again. And for a third time he prayed for the same thing.
Then he returned to the disciples. He said to them, "Are you still sleeping and taking your rest? Look! The hour is already near. The Son of Man is about to be betrayed into the hands of sinners."
Arise! Let us go! Behold, he who betrays me is at hand!" Jesus is arrested
While Jesus was still speaking, Judas arrived. He was one of the twelve. A large crowd was with him, carrying swords and clubs. The chief priests and elders of the people had sent them.
Judah, who was going to hand Jesus over, had arranged a signal with them. He said, "The man I kiss is the one; seize him."
Judas immediately went to Jesus. He said, "Greetings, Rabbi!" and kissed him.
Jesus answered, "My friend, do as you have come for." Then the men approached. They seized Jesus and arrested him.
At that moment, one of Jesus' disciples reached for his sword. He drew it and struck the high priest's servant, cutting off his ear.
Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back in its place. For all who take the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you think I can't ask my father for help? He would send an army of more than 70,000 angels immediately.
But how would the scriptures be fulfilled then? They say it must happen this way."
At that time Jesus spoke to the crowd, "Shall I lead an armed band of men against you?" he asked. "Should you come out with swords and cudgels to arrest me? Every day I used to sit in the temple courts teaching. Yet you did not arrest me."
But all this has happened so that the words of the prophets may be fulfilled." And all the disciples left him and fled. Jesus is taken to the Sanhedrin
Those who had arrested Jesus brought him to Caiaphas, the high priest. The teachers of the law and the elders were assembled there.
Not too far away, Peter followed Jesus. He went straight to the courtyard of the chief priest. He entered and sat down with the guards to see what would happen.
The chief priests and the entire Sanhedrin were searching for something they could use against Jesus. They wanted to put him to death.
But they didn't find any evidence, even though many false witnesses came forward. Finally, two more witnesses came forward.
They said, "This man claims, 'I am able to destroy the temple of God and rebuild it in three days.'"
Then the high priest stood up. He asked Jesus, "Will you not answer? What are these charges that these men are bringing against you?"
But Jesus remained silent. The high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God, tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God."
"Yes. Exactly as you say," Jesus answered. "But this is what I tell you all. In the coming days, you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of the Mighty One. And you will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the High Priest tore his clothes. "He has spoken a very evil word against God!" he exclaimed. "Why do we need more witnesses? You have heard him speak this evil word.
"What do you think?" "He must die!" they answered.
Then they spat in his face. They hit him with their fists. And others slapped him.
They said, "Prophesy to us, Christ! Who is it that struck you?" Peter says that he does not know Jesus.
Peter was sitting in the courtyard; a servant girl came to him and said, "You too were with Jesus the Galilean."
But in front of everyone, Peter said that he was not. "I don't know what you are talking about," he said.
Then he went out to the gate leading to the courtyard. There another woman saw him. She said to the people, "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth."
Again he said he had not been. With an oath he cried: "I do not know the man!"
Soon, those who were standing there approached Peter. They said, "Surely you are one of them; your accent betrays you."
Then Peter began to curse himself. He swore to them and said, "I do not know this man!" And immediately a cock crowed.
Then Peter remembered what Jesus had said to him, "The cock will not crow, until you have denied Me three times." Peter went out and wept bitterly.
Judas hangs himself.
It was early in the morning. All the chief priests and the elders of the people decided to put Jesus to death.
They bound him and took him away. Then they delivered him to Pilate, who was the governor.
Judas, who had betrayed him, saw that Jesus was condemned to death. He felt deep remorse and sorrow for what he had done. So he took the thirty pieces of silver back to the chief priests and elders.
He said, "I made a mistake. I handed over a man who is not guilty." They replied, "What concern is it of ours? This is your problem."
Then Judas threw the money into the temple and left. Then he went and hanged himself.
The priests picked up the coins. "It is against the law," they said, "to put this money in the Temple treasury. It is blood money. It has paid for a man's death."
So they decided to use the money to buy a potter's field. People from other countries will be buried there.
This is why the Field of Blood has been called so until this day.
Then the words of Jeremiah the prophet came true. He had said, "They took the thirty pieces of silver. It was the price their god put on his head. The people of Israel paid it to them to buy the potter's field, as the Lord had commanded."
They used the pieces of silver to buy the potter's field, as the Lord commanded me." (Zechariah 11:12, 13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9) Jesus is Brought to Pilate
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is as you say."
But when the chief priests and elders brought charges against him, he made no response.
Then Pilate asked him, "Don't you hear the charges they are bringing against you?"
But Jesus answered not a word to any of the charges. The governor was exceedingly astonished.
It was the custom of the Governor on Easter Day to pardon one prisoner chosen by the people.
At that time they had a notorious prisoner called Barabbas.
When the crowd had gathered, Pilate addressed them, saying, "Which one do you want me to release for you: Barabbas, or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were jealous. He knew that this was why they had handed Jesus over to him.
While Pilate was sitting on the judge's seat, his wife sent him a message. In it she said, "Don't get involved with that man. He is not guilty. I have suffered greatly in a dream today because of him."
But the chief priests and elders incited the crowd to ask for Barabbas to be released and Jesus to be executed.
"Which do you want me to release?" asked the governor. "Barabbas," they answered.
Pilate asked, "What then shall I do with Jesus, who is called the Christ?" And all the people answered, "Crucify him!"
"Why? What has he done wrong?" Pilate asked. But they shouted all the louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that he was making no progress. Instead, the crowd began to grow angry. He took water and washed his hands before them. "I am innocent of this man's blood," he said. "You are responsible!" they shouted.
"We and our children will accept the guilt for his death!" all the people answered.
Barabbas was released by Pilate. However, he ordered Jesus to be flogged. Then he handed him over to be crucified on the cross. The soldiers mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which was called the Praetorium. All the other soldiers gathered around him.
They stripped him and dressed him in a purple robe.
Then they twisted the thorns together to make a crown. They put it on his head. They put a staff in his right hand. Then they fell down on their knees in front of him and mocked him. "We honor you, O King of the Jews!" they said.
They spat on him. They hit him on the head with a stick again and again.
After mocking him, they took off his robe. They returned his own clothes to him. Then they led him away to crucify him on the cross. Jesus is crucified on the cross.
On their way out of the city, they met a man from Cyrene. His name was Simon. They forced him to carry the cross.
They came to a place called Golgotha. The word Golgotha means the place of the skull.
There they mixed the wine with bitter spices and gave it to Jesus to drink. And when he tasted it, he refused to drink it.
When they crucified him on the cross, they divided his garments by casting lots.
They sat and guarded him there.
Above his head they placed the written accusation against him. It was written: ~This is Jesus, the King of the Jews.~
Two robbers were crucified with him; one was at his right and the other at his left.
Those who passed by scoffed at Jesus and ridiculed him. They shook their heads
And he said, "Will you destroy the temple and build it in three days? Then save yourself! Come down from the cross, if you are the son of God!"
In the same way, the chief priests, the teachers of the law and the elders ridiculed him.
"They saved others," they said. "But he cannot save himself! He is the King of Israel! Let him come down from the cross now! Then we will believe in him."
He trusts in God. Let God save him now if He wills. It is He who said, "I am the Son of God."
In the same way, the robbers who were crucified with Jesus also mocked him. Jesus dies
From noon until three o'clock, the entire earth was covered in darkness.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, " 'Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani?' " This means, " 'My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?' " (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those who stood there heard Jesus cry out. "He is calling Elijah," they said.
Immediately someone ran and got a sponge. He filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed. He offered Jesus a drink to drink.
The rest said, "Leave him alone. Let us see if Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
In that moment, the curtain of the temple was torn from top to bottom into two parts. The earth shook. The rocks were split.
The tombs were opened. And the bodies of many saints who had died were raised to life.
They came out of the tombs. After Jesus rose from the dead, they entered the holy city. There they appeared to many people.
The Roman centurion and those who were guarding Jesus saw the earthquake and all that had happened; and they were filled with awe and said, "Truly, he was the Son of God!"
Not too far away, many women were watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
Mary Magdalene was one of them. Mary, the mother of James and Joseph, was there also. And the mother of the sons of Zebedee. Jesus was buried
As evening approached, a wealthy man from the city of Arimathea came. His name was Joseph. He had become a follower of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for the body of Jesus. Pilate commanded that he should be given it.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in pure linen cloth.
He placed him in his new grave, which he had excavated from the rock himself. He rolled a large stone in front of the entrance to the grave. Then he left.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb. The guards at the tomb
The next day was the one after the Preparation Day. The chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
"Sir," they said, "we recall something the deceiver said while he was still alive. He claimed, 'After three days I will rise again.'"
Then order the tomb to be secured until the third day. If you do not do this, then his disciples may come and steal the body. Then they will tell the people that Jesus has risen from the dead. This last lie will be more powerful than the first one.
Pilate said, "Take with you some guards. Go. Make the tomb as secure as you can."
So they went and securely locked the tomb. They placed a seal on the stone and appointed some guards for the watch.
Jesus rises from the dead.
Saturday is now past. The dawn came on the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to look at the tomb.
There was a strong earthquake. The Angel of the Lord descended from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. He rolled away the stone and sat on it.
His body was shining like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like the dead.
The angel said to the women, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus, who was crucified."
He is not here! He has risen, just as he said! Come and see the place where he lay.
Go quickly! Tell his disciples, "He has been raised from the dead. He is going ahead of you to Galilee. There you will see him." Now I have told you.
The women hurried away from the tomb. They were afraid, but they were filled with joy. They ran to tell the disciples.
Suddenly they met Jesus. He said, "Peace be with you!" They came to him, held his feet and worshipped him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee. There they will see me." The guards' report to the chief priests.
While the women were on their way, some guards entered the city. They reported to the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the chief priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They gave a large sum of money to the soldiers.
They said to the soldiers: "We want you to say: 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the governor hears this report, we will pay him off. This will save you from trouble.
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were instructed. This story has been spread among Jews until this day. The Final Instructions of Jesus to His Disciples
Then the eleven disciples went to Galilee. They went to the mountain that Jesus had told them to go to.
When they saw him, they worshipped him. But some still had doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me.
Go therefore and make disciples of all nations. Baptize them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey everything I have commanded you. And be sure that I am always with you, to the very end.
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote, "I will send my messenger before you. He will prepare your way." (Malachi 3:1)
A voice cries out in the wilderness: "Prepare the way of the Lord. Make straight paths for him in the desert." (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. He was baptizing people in the desert. He also preached that people should be baptized and repent of their sins. Then God would forgive them.
All the people from the countryside of Judea went out to him. All the people from Jerusalem went out as well. When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John was wearing clothes made of camel's hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. And he was eating locusts and wild honey.
This is what John used to preach. "After me, one will come who is mightier than I. I am not worthy to stoop down and untie the thong of his sandals."
I baptize you with water. But he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Jesus is baptized and tempted.
At that time Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. John baptized him in the Jordan River.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. At that moment, he saw the heavens were split open. He saw the Holy Spirit come down on him like a dove.
A voice from heaven spoke to him, saying, "You are my son, whom I love. I am very pleased with you."
Immediately the Holy Spirit sent Jesus to the wilderness.
He was in the desert for forty days. There the devil tempted him. Wild animals did not harm Jesus. Angels took care of him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John was put in prison, Jesus went into Galilee. He taught God's good news.
He said, "The time has come. The Kingdom of God is near. Repent of your sins and believe in the good news!"
One day Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were throwing a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow me, and I will make you fishers of people."
So they left their nets and followed him immediately.
Then Jesus walked a little further. As he did this, he saw James son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat preparing their nets.
Immediately he called them. They left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men. Then they followed Jesus. Jesus Expels an Evil Spirit
Jesus and those who were with him went to Capernaum. And when the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. There he began to teach.
People were astonished at his teaching. He taught them with authority. He did not speak as the teachers of the law did.
At that moment a man in their synagogue cried out. He was being controlled by an evil spirit. He said,
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One of God!
"Be quiet!" Jesus said firmly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him with a scream.
And all the people were astonished. And they asked one another, "What is this? A new teaching! And with authority! He commands even the evil spirits, and they obey him."
The news of Jesus spread rapidly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
Jesus and those who were with him left the synagogue. Immediately they went, along with James and John, to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand and helped her up. The fever left her. Then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all the sick. They also brought all who were possessed by demons.
All the people of the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They were suffering from all kinds of diseases. He also cast out many evil spirits. But he did not allow the spirits to speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prays in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning, and still dark. Jesus got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they called out to him, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to some other place. I want to go to the nearby towns. I must preach there also. For this reason I have come."
He wandered all over Galilee. He taught in their synagogues, and he cast out demons. Jesus heals a man with a skin disease.
A man who was suffering from a skin disease came to Jesus. On his knees, he begged Jesus. He said, "If you want to make me clean, you can do it."
Jesus was filled with deep concern. He reached out his hand and touched the man. "I am willing to do this," he said. "Be clean!"
Immediately the illness left him. He was cured.
Jesus immediately sent him away. He gave the man a strong warning.
"Don't tell anyone about this," he said. "Go and show yourself to the priest. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. It will be a testimony to the priest and to the people that you are 'clean'."
But the man went out and began to talk at once. He spread the news to everyone. So Jesus could no longer enter the city publicly. He stayed outside in remote places. But people still came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. The people heard that he had returned to his homeland.
A great multitude of people gathered together, so that there was no room for them, not even outside the door. And Jesus spoke to them the word.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they could not bring him to Jesus because of the crowd. So they made an opening in the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man on his mat through the opening.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some of the teachers of the law were sitting there, reasoning within themselves,
Why is this man talking like this? He's saying something very wicked! Only God forgives sins!
Immediately Jesus knew what they were thinking in their hearts. And he said to them, "Why are you thinking these things?
Is it easier to say to this man, 'Your sins are forgiven you'? or to say, 'Rise, take up your bed and walk'?
"I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
He said to him, "I tell you, get up. Take your mat and go to your house."
The man got up and took his mat. Then he walked away, while everyone was watching. All the people were amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this before!" Jesus Chooses Levi
Jesus went out again beside the Sea of Galilee. A great multitude came to him. He began to teach them.
While he was walking, he saw Levi, the son of Alphaeus. Levi was sitting at the tax collector's booth. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And Levi rose and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at the home of Levi. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that followed Jesus.
There were some teachers of the law who were Pharisees. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard that. He said to them, "Those who are in good health do not need a physician, but the sick do need one. I did not come to call those who think they are righteous before God to follow me, but I have come to call sinners to follow me." Jesus is asked about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting. Some people came to Jesus and said to him, "The disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees also fast. But your disciples are not fasting. Why are they not fasting?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them.
But a time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. On that day they will go away without eating.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto old clothes. If they did this, the new piece would tear away from the old one. This would make the tear even worse.
People do not put new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wineskins will burst, spilling the wine and destroying the wineskins. Instead, everyone puts new wine into new wineskins. This preserves both the wine and the wineskins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Saturday, Jesus was walking with his disciples through the grain fields. The disciples began to pick some heads of grain.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look! This man is doing what is unlawful on the Sabbath. Why then do your disciples do it?"
He answered, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
This was when Abiathar was the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate the bread of consecration. Only the priests were permitted to eat it. David also gave some of the bread to his men.
Then Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath.
For the Son of Man is Lord even of the sabbath day.
Healing on Saturday
Jesus went back to the synagogue. There was a man with a weak, bent hand.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They watched him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man who had the withered and crooked hand. He said to him, "Stand up in front of everyone."
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the law say we must do on the Sabbath? Should we do good? Or should we do evil? Should we save life? Or should we kill?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them in anger, deeply distressed because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out, and his hand was restored like the hand of a new-born child.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, seeking to kill him. The crowds follow Jesus.
Jesus went to the Sea of Galilee with his disciples. Great crowds followed him from Galilee.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing. Many people came to him. They came from Judea, Jerusalem, and Edom. They came from the territories east of the Jordan River. And they came from the area around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus told his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This would prevent people from crowding around him.
Jesus healed many people. Therefore, the sick were pushing to touch him.
When the people who had evil spirits saw him, they fell down in front of him. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up on a mountainside. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
He appointed twelve of them and called them apostles. And from then on, they were with him. He also sent them out to preach.
They had the power to cast out demons.
Jesus appointed the twelve. Simon was one of them. And Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
And there were also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And there were also Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them too. He was the one who would later deliver Jesus into the hands of his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. A crowd gathered again. It was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about this. So they went to take his order. They said, "He is out of his mind."
There were some Pharisees. They came down from Jerusalem. They said, "He is controlled by Beelzebul! He casts out demons by the power of the prince of demons."
He called them, saying, "Come here," and He spoke to them in parables. He said, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom is at war with itself, it cannot endure.
If a family divides, it cannot endure.
